From: ane-stack
Subject: [PATCH 0017] espresso: decode macOS 27.x's nested loadModel code

Patch 0014's take_code grabbed the first 0x token in the description. That is
correct on macOS 26.x, where the code is a terminal parenthesised token and the
only hex token present. It is WRONG on macOS 27.x, whose message shape is:

  ...error:: Program load failed — bad program image (model may be corrupt or
  incompatible) (underlying=0x13)

There the code is nested after underlying=. Measured on a real macOS 27.0 box:
the old rule returned Some(19) and classified a genuine deploy refusal as
Unrecognised with form=None, i.e. it silently discarded the code.

Resolution order: underlying=0x... first (unambiguous), then a terminal
parenthesised (0x...), then the first hex token as a last resort.

Also corrects a test that encoded the old assumption. only_the_first_hex_
token_is_read asserted the first token wins; that assumption predates 27.x and
gets 26.x-style descriptions wrong once an incidental earlier hex token is
present. Renamed to parenthesised_trailing_code_beats_earlier_hex_token, with
the reason recorded inline per ENGINEERING.md 3.1 — the original intent
(selector text must not win) is subsumed by the corrected rule.

Both message shapes are pinned by tests, since they are indistinguishable
without OS provenance.

Regression-checked on macOS 26.7.1: all four previously-measured codes still
decode (0x170004 x2, 0x130004 x2). On macOS 27.0 the code now reads 0x13.
Lands on Unrecognised still, because the 27.x cause text is new and not in the
measured table — deliberate, and documented on the enum.

Evidence: docs/evidence/PROBE-2-macos27-result.md
